.md
## Workers API
WLJS now provides asynchronous access to parallel kernels. The freeware Wolfram Engine is not limited to a single parallel kernel or subkernel, so you can use this API to build truly parallel Wolfram Language workflows.

> Why not `ParallelSubmit`?

The default Wolfram parallel API is blocking, which makes it hard to stream data in and out without pausing the main kernel. The Workers API avoids that bottleneck. Here is a basic message loopback example:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
## Workers API
WLJS now provides asynchronous access to parallel kernels. The freeware Wolfram Engine is not limited to a single parallel kernel or subkernel, so you can use this API to build truly parallel Wolfram Language workflows.

> Why not `ParallelSubmit`?

The default Wolfram parallel API is blocking, which makes it hard to stream data in and out without pausing the main kernel. The Workers API avoids that bottleneck. Here is a basic message loopback example:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
w = WorkerLaunch[Module[{},
  EventHandler[Null, {
    _ -> (EventFire[Null, #]&)
  }]
]]

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
WorkerObject[(*VB[*) LinkObject["'/Applications/Wolfram Engine.app/Contents/Resources/Wolfram Player.app/Contents/MacOS/WolframKernel' -noicon -wstp -noinit -subkernel", 819, 11], "dfaaf4aa-19b6-484b-8ce0-ef7d3dc13233" (*,*)(*"1:"*)(*]VB*)]

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.md
You can also start a worker from a `.wl` file:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
You can also start a worker from a `.wl` file:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
w = WorkerLaunch["path_to_worker.wl"];

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.md
Each worker object runs in a separate OS thread and communicates with the main kernel through the shared-memory WSTP protocol.

This design mirrors the JavaScript Workers API used across frontend and backend systems. In the example below, we subscribe to messages from a worker, then submit one to it:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Each worker object runs in a separate OS thread and communicates with the main kernel through the shared-memory WSTP protocol.

This design mirrors the JavaScript Workers API used across frontend and backend systems. In the example below, we subscribe to messages from a worker, then submit one to it:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
EventHandler[w, Print];
EventFire[w, "Hey!"];

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.md
Terminate the worker when it is no longer needed:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Terminate the worker when it is no longer needed:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
WorkerClose[w]

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.md
Check the documentation for more examples.

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
